WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.061
Input validation is the point where external data crosses into trusted program logic.

2
00:00:05.061 --> 00:00:12.847
In C++, that boundary deserves special attention because raw bytes, strings, counts, indexes, configuration values, and records can quickly influence

3
00:00:12.847 --> 00:00:16.350
memory layout, object state, resource use, and control flow.

4
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.022
External input does not come only from forms or command-line prompts.

5
00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:28.029
It can arrive through files, command-line arguments, environment variables, network messages, configuration, plugins, API responses, databases, build

6
00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:29.586
metadata, and other components.

7
00:00:29.586 --> 00:00:39.708
A value may look internal simply because another service or library handed it to your code, but it still needs a contract before it becomes trusted

8
00:00:39.708 --> 00:00:40.097
state.

9
00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:42.822
Validation is not just a formatting step.

10
00:00:42.822 --> 00:00:45.158
It is a boundary-control decision.

11
00:00:45.158 --> 00:00:52.555
The program decides whether the data has the expected shape, type, range, encoding, size, and relationship to other values.

12
00:00:52.555 --> 00:00:58.783
If the answer is no, the program should fail predictably before the value reaches deeper logic.

13
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:03.066
A reliable design keeps raw input separate from trusted internal values.

14
00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:12.019
Raw text or bytes can be stored in clearly named variables and converted into narrow domain types only after parsing and validation succeed.

15
00:01:12.019 --> 00:01:15.134
That separation helps reviewers see where trust changes.

16
00:01:15.134 --> 00:01:18.248
Validation also supports memory safety and resource safety.

17
00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:20.584
Counts should be bounded before allocation.

18
00:01:20.584 --> 00:01:22.920
Indexes should be checked before use.

19
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.866
Text should be interpreted consistently.

20
00:01:24.866 --> 00:01:27.591
Structured records should be validated before conversion.

21
00:01:27.591 --> 00:01:33.041
When validation is explicit, the rest of the program can operate on clearer assumptions.

22
00:01:33.041 --> 00:01:40.048
For review, ask where input first enters, what contract it must satisfy, and where the trusted representation begins.

23
00:01:40.048 --> 00:01:44.720
Strong C++ validation is a design habit, not a last-minute filter.
